			<div class="content" id="post-content-4792403">
									

<p>Draft: <a href="http://scp-sandbox-3.wikidot.com/limeyy">http://scp-sandbox-3.wikidot.com/limeyy</a></p>
<p>Greenlight: <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-12195737/idea-not-sure-if-it-s-been-done-before">http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-12195737/idea-not-sure-if-it-s-been-done-before</a></p>
<p>Hey all :)</p>
<p>Starting from the idea that came up in passing ages ago in IRC - 'can you imagine how much paperwork the foundation has to deal with?', I've written an scp based around an investigation into a foundation site, using secondary and tertiary evidence to work out when an object that really doesn't want to be found ended up in a foundation site.</p>
<p>Coming back to it in bursts months later, I think I've got a relatively clean first draft, but I'm having trouble making sure an scp that's based around reading emails and lots of walls of text isn't overstaying it's welcome - I've had to cut the investigation down as is to get it to the 2k words it's at, although now I'm worried a bit that it's all a little rushed.</p>
<p>Concerns:</p>
<p>-Overstaying it's welcome (as above)</p>
<p>-The investigation/narrative does not draw people in (this was a concern found in thecritters, and I have added the first email in addendum to try and solve this, but I don't know if that email is too OTT or doesn't add much to solve this)</p>
<p>-It stumbles the ending without a satisfying payoff after all the buildup (my main worry after polishing it a bit)</p>
<p>If there are still spag or tone errors, I apologise - I've tried to get the worst of them but this is my first bit of long form writing in a couple years, so I'm bound to have missed bits and pieces.</p>
<p>Thanks for your time reading this far :)</p>

